<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pir Panjal Range</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pir Panjal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pir Panjal Range is a chain of the Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> that separates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> valley from the hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> plains. Its passes, above all the Pir Panjal Pass on the Mughal road, carried traffic into Kashmir for centuries, and small hill states such as Lohara and Rajauri held the approaches to the valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pir-panjal-range.jpg" alt="Pir Panjal Range" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Just Jimish · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pir_Panjal_2478293509_8000ae5902_o.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Pir Panjal Range is a range of the Lower Himalaya in the Western Himalayas, running from south-east to north-west between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> and the Neelam or Kishanganga rivers, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, with its north-western end extending into territory administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. It is the largest and westernmost range of the Lesser Himalayas.</p><p>Near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> it separates from the main Himalayan range and forms a divide between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> rivers on one side and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a> on the other. Further west it forms the south-western boundary of the Kashmir Valley, dividing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a> from the Chenab. Deo Tibba, at 6,001 metres, and Indrasan, at 6,221 metres, are two important peaks at its eastern end, and the hill station of Gulmarg lies in the range.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The range is named after the Pir Panjal Pass, whose original name as recorded by Srivara was Panchaladeva, meaning the deity of Panchala. Panchala is a country mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>. The scholar M. A. Stein believed that the idea of a deity was translated into that of a pir after the region was Islamised.</p><p>Passes across the range have long linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> with its neighbours. The Pir Panjal Pass, also called Peer Ki Gali, connects the Kashmir Valley with Rajouri and Poonch by the Mughal Road, of which it is the highest point at 3,490 metres. The Banihal Pass, at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>, lies at the south end of the valley, and the Sinthan Pass links the valley with Kishtwar.</p><p>The Haji Pir Pass, between Poonch and Uri, was taken twice by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in military operations, in 1948 and 1965, and each time left under Pakistani control. In the east the Rohtang La connects Manali in the Kullu Valley to Keylong in the Lahaul Valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tunnels now carry traffic under the passes. 85 kilometres long, was built in the early 1950s and commissioned in December 1956 to give snow-free passage under the Banihal Pass all year. It was designed for 150 vehicles a day and later carried more than 7,000. 45 kilometres, was started in 2011, commissioned in 2021 and shortened the road between the two towns by 16 kilometres.</p><p>8 kilometres long, runs under the Rohtang Pass and cut about 60 kilometres from the journey between Manali and Keylong. 215 kilometres between Qazigund and Banihal was commissioned on 26 June 2013 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udhampur">Udhampur</a>–Srinagar–Baramulla railway project.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pir Panjal Range” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1003 Queen Didda of the Parvagupta dynasty was succeeded by her nephew Sangramaraja, a prince of Lohara, a hill state in the <b>Pir Panjal</b> range.</blockquote><a href="/state/lohara-dynasty">Lohara dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Rajauri controlled a stage on the Mughal road over the <b>Pir Panjal</b> to Kashmir, which the emperors used on their journeys to the valley; Mughal gardens and inns survive along it.</blockquote><a href="/state/rajauri-state">Rajauri State</a></li></ul></section>
